{
  "title": "Arrays vs lists",
  "summary": "Why NumPy exists, and your first arrays.",
  "minutes": 10,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Why NumPy?",
      "body": [
        "A Python list holds *references to objects*, and `*`, `+` mean list things (repeat, join).",
        "A NumPy **array** holds raw numbers packed side by side in memory, all of one type, and",
        "arithmetic on it works **element by element**:",
        "",
        "```python",
        "import numpy as np",
        "",
        "prices = [10, 20, 30]",
        "prices * 2                 # [10, 20, 30, 10, 20, 30]  (repeats the list)",
        "",
        "a = np.array(prices)",
        "a * 2                      # array([20, 40, 60])",
        "a + np.array([1, 2, 3])    # array([11, 22, 33])",
        "```",
        "",
        "Every library in this course (pandas, matplotlib, scikit-learn later) is built on these",
        "arrays. Writing `a * 2` instead of a loop is called **vectorised** code: shorter, and it",
        "runs in compiled C instead of the Python interpreter."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "import numpy as np",
        "",
        "xs = [1, 2, 3]",
        "a = np.array(xs)",
        "print(xs * 2)",
        "print((a * 2).tolist())",
        "print((a + a).tolist())",
        "print(xs + xs)"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "[1, 2, 3, 1, 2, 3]",
        "[2, 4, 6]",
        "[2, 4, 6]",
        "[1, 2, 3, 1, 2, 3]"
      ],
      "explain": "On lists, `*` repeats and `+` concatenates. On arrays both are element-wise maths. `.tolist()` turns an array back into a plain list."
    },
    {
      "type": "mcq",
      "prompt": "You have `a = np.array([1, 2, 3])` and `b = np.array([10, 20, 30])`. What is `a * b`?",
      "options": [
        "`array([10, 40, 90])`",
        "`140` (the dot product)",
        "`[1, 2, 3, 10, 20, 30]`",
        "a `TypeError`"
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explain": "`*` multiplies matching positions. The dot product is `a @ b` or `np.dot(a, b)`."
    },
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Making arrays",
      "body": [
        "You rarely type arrays by hand. The common constructors:",
        "",
        "```python",
        "np.array([1, 2, 3])          # from a list",
        "np.arange(0, 10, 2)          # like range: [0, 2, 4, 6, 8]   (stop excluded)",
        "np.linspace(0, 1, 5)         # 5 evenly spaced points: [0, .25, .5, .75, 1]  (stop included)",
        "np.zeros(3)                  # [0., 0., 0.]",
        "np.ones(3)                   # [1., 1., 1.]",
        "np.full(3, 7)                # [7, 7, 7]",
        "```",
        "",
        "`arange` is for steps of a known size, `linspace` for a known number of points",
        "(great for chart axes)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "match",
      "prompt": "Match each call to the list it produces (via `.tolist()`).",
      "pairs": [
        [
          "`np.arange(1, 5)`",
          "`[1, 2, 3, 4]`"
        ],
        [
          "`np.linspace(1, 5, 3)`",
          "`[1.0, 3.0, 5.0]`"
        ],
        [
          "`np.zeros(2)`",
          "`[0.0, 0.0]`"
        ],
        [
          "`np.full(3, 4)`",
          "`[4, 4, 4]`"
        ],
        [
          "`np.arange(10, 0, -3)`",
          "`[10, 7, 4, 1]`"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "import numpy as np",
        "",
        "print(np.arange(2, 12, 3).tolist())",
        "print(np.linspace(0, 10, 3).tolist())",
        "print(len(np.arange(0, 1, 0.25)))"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "[2, 5, 8, 11]",
        "[0.0, 5.0, 10.0]",
        "4"
      ],
      "explain": "`arange` stops before `stop` (11 is the last one before 12 here: 2, 5, 8, 11). `linspace` includes both ends."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "title": "Celsius to Fahrenheit",
      "prompt": [
        "Write `to_fahrenheit(celsius)` that takes a **list** of temperatures in °C and returns a",
        "NumPy **array** in °F, using `F = C * 9 / 5 + 32`.",
        "",
        "Do it without a loop or comprehension: convert to an array, then do the maths once.",
        "",
        "```python",
        "to_fahrenheit([0, 100, -40])   # array([ 32., 212., -40.])",
        "```"
      ],
      "starter": "import numpy as np\n\n\ndef to_fahrenheit(celsius):\n    pass\n",
      "solution": [
        "import numpy as np",
        "",
        "",
        "def to_fahrenheit(celsius):",
        "    c = np.array(celsius)",
        "    return c * 9 / 5 + 32"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "`c = np.array(celsius)` first.",
        "Then `return c * 9 / 5 + 32`."
      ],
      "template": [
        "{{CODE}}",
        "import numpy as _np",
        "_r = to_fahrenheit([0, 100, -40])",
        "Dojo.check(\"returns a NumPy array\", isinstance(_r, _np.ndarray), \"got \" + type(_r).__name__)",
        "Dojo.eq(\"[0, 100, -40] -> [32, 212, -40]\", [32.0, 212.0, -40.0], _np.asarray(_r, dtype=float).round(2).tolist())",
        "Dojo.eq(\"[36.6] -> [97.88]\", [97.88], _np.asarray(to_fahrenheit([36.6]), dtype=float).round(2).tolist())"
      ],
      "forbid": [
        {
          "pattern": "\\bfor\\b",
          "message": "No loop or comprehension"
        },
        {
          "pattern": "\\bwhile\\b",
          "message": "No while loop"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "blanks",
      "prompt": "Total the order lines, vectorised.",
      "code": [
        "import numpy as np",
        "",
        "qty = np.__1__([2, 1, 5])",
        "price = np.array([3.5, 10.0, 1.2])",
        "line_totals = qty __2__ price",
        "print(line_totals.__3__())"
      ],
      "blanks": [
        "array",
        "*",
        "sum"
      ],
      "bank": [
        "list",
        "@",
        "+",
        "len",
        "total"
      ],
      "explain": "Element-wise `*` gives each line's total; `.sum()` adds them up (23.0)."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "title": "Order totals with a discount",
      "prompt": [
        "Write `order_total(qty, price, discount)`:",
        "",
        "- `qty` and `price` are lists of the same length (one entry per order line),",
        "- `discount` is a fraction, e.g. `0.1` for 10% off **every** line,",
        "- return the grand total as a plain `float`, rounded to 2 decimals.",
        "",
        "```python",
        "order_total([2, 1], [5.0, 10.0], 0.1)   # 18.0",
        "```",
        "",
        "Use arrays: no loops."
      ],
      "starter": "import numpy as np\n\n\ndef order_total(qty, price, discount):\n    pass\n",
      "solution": [
        "import numpy as np",
        "",
        "",
        "def order_total(qty, price, discount):",
        "    lines = np.array(qty) * np.array(price) * (1 - discount)",
        "    return round(float(lines.sum()), 2)"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "Multiply the two arrays, then by `(1 - discount)`.",
        "`round(float(lines.sum()), 2)`"
      ],
      "template": [
        "{{CODE}}",
        "Dojo.eq(\"two lines, 10% off\", 18.0, order_total([2, 1], [5.0, 10.0], 0.1))",
        "Dojo.eq(\"no discount\", 23.0, order_total([2, 1, 5], [3.5, 10.0, 1.2], 0))",
        "Dojo.eq(\"25% off\", 7.5, order_total([1, 1], [4.0, 6.0], 0.25))",
        "Dojo.check(\"returns a float\", isinstance(order_total([1], [1.0], 0), float), \"wrap the result in float(...)\")"
      ],
      "forbid": [
        {
          "pattern": "\\bfor\\b",
          "message": "No loop or comprehension"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "title": "A week of temperatures",
      "prompt": [
        "Write `week_summary(temps)` that takes a list of daily temperatures and returns a dict:",
        "",
        "```python",
        "week_summary([18, 21, 25, 19, 23, 30, 17])",
        "# {\"mean\": 21.86, \"min\": 17, \"max\": 30, \"range\": 13}",
        "```",
        "",
        "- `mean` rounded to 2 decimals (a `float`),",
        "- `min`, `max` and `range` (max - min) as plain numbers.",
        "",
        "Use array methods (`.mean()`, `.min()`, `.max()`) and convert results with `float()` / `int()`",
        "or `round()` so the tests see plain Python numbers."
      ],
      "starter": "import numpy as np\n\n\ndef week_summary(temps):\n    t = np.array(temps)\n",
      "solution": [
        "import numpy as np",
        "",
        "",
        "def week_summary(temps):",
        "    t = np.array(temps)",
        "    return {",
        "        \"mean\": round(float(t.mean()), 2),",
        "        \"min\": t.min().item(),",
        "        \"max\": t.max().item(),",
        "        \"range\": (t.max() - t.min()).item(),",
        "    }"
      ],
      "hints": [
        "`t.mean()`, `t.min()`, `t.max()` are all methods on the array.",
        "`.item()` turns a NumPy scalar into a plain Python number; `round(float(t.mean()), 2)` for the mean."
      ],
      "template": [
        "{{CODE}}",
        "Dojo.eq(\"example week\", {\"mean\": 21.86, \"min\": 17, \"max\": 30, \"range\": 13}, week_summary([18, 21, 25, 19, 23, 30, 17]))",
        "Dojo.eq(\"flat week\", {\"mean\": 5.0, \"min\": 5, \"max\": 5, \"range\": 0}, week_summary([5, 5, 5]))",
        "Dojo.eq(\"decimals\", {\"mean\": 1.5, \"min\": 0.5, \"max\": 2.5, \"range\": 2.0}, week_summary([0.5, 1.5, 2.5]))"
      ],
      "forbid": [
        {
          "pattern": "\\bfor\\b",
          "message": "No loop or comprehension"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
